```java
public class SharedStateDemo {
    private static int shared; // class state reachable by both threads

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Runnable task = () -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1_000; i++) {
                shared++;       // unsafe compound update
            }
        };
        Thread first = new Thread(task);
        Thread second = new Thread(task);
        first.start();
        second.start();
        first.join();
        second.join();
        System.out.println(shared); // 2,000 is not guaranteed
    }
}
```

Both threads can reach the same class state because they run in one process. A separate process cannot access this Java variable directly; cross-process communication needs an operating-system or external mechanism, which can include explicitly shared memory.

```java
public class MainThreadDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // This code runs in the main thread
        System.out.println("Main thread: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());

        // Spawn a non-daemon thread
        var workerThread = new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                Thread.sleep(3000);
                System.out.println("Worker finished after main");
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        });
        workerThread.start(); // Non-daemon by default

        // Spawn a daemon thread
        var daemonThread = new Thread(() -> {
            while (true) {
                System.out.println("Daemon running...");
                try { Thread.sleep(500); } catch (InterruptedException e) { break; }
            }
        });
        daemonThread.setDaemon(true); // Mark as daemon BEFORE start()
        daemonThread.start();

        System.out.println("Main method ending");
        // JVM waits for workerThread but kills daemonThread
    }
}
```

```java
