t.setDaemon(true); // IllegalThreadStateException — thread already started
```

```java
ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(workerCount);
Future<Result> first = executor.submit(() -> loadPart(1));
Future<Result> second = executor.submit(() -> loadPart(2));

Result combined = combine(first.get(), second.get());
executor.shutdown();
```

This example makes two independent loads eligible to overlap and explicitly observes both results. A real application must choose `workerCount`, timeouts, cancellation, queue policy, and executor ownership from the workload; a framework's request executor is configuration-dependent.

```java
// Spawns a fresh OS thread for every task — exhausts memory at scale
for (Order order : pendingOrders) {
    new Thread(() -> orderService.processPayment(order)).start();
}
```

```java
// Reuse a fixed set of threads via ExecutorService
ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Math.max(1, Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors()));
for (Order order : pendingOrders) {
    pool.submit(() -> orderService.processPayment(order));
}
pool.shutdown();
```
*A fixed worker set reuses threads, but its queue must also be bounded deliberately instead of creating thousands, keeping memory and context switching under control.*

```java
class UnsafeCounter {
    private int count = 0;

    public void increment() {
        count++; // read, add, write — NOT atomic
    }

    public int getCount() { return count; }
}
// Two threads incrementing concurrently lose updates.
```

```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

class SafeCounter {
    private final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);

    public void increment() {
        count.incrementAndGet(); // lock-free atomic update
    }

    public int getCount() { return count.get(); }
}
```
*`AtomicInteger` turns the read-add-write into one atomic step, so no update is lost. A `synchronized` method would also work.*

```java
int processors = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();
for (int workers : List.of(1, processors, processors * 2)) {
    runRepresentativeLoad(workers);
}
```

Treat processor count as one starting point for a CPU-heavy workload, not an answer. A meaningful test needs representative tasks, warmup, repeated measurements, resource monitoring, and controlled inputs; JMH is suitable for isolated JVM benchmarks, while application load tests are needed for end-to-end tuning.

```java
// 500 threads for CPU-heavy work on a few cores = constant switching
ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(500);
```

```java
// CPU-bound: match the pool to the available cores
int cores = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();
ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(cores);
```
*For CPU-bound work, a pool near the core count is a useful measurement starting point. I/O-bound work can use a larger pool since threads mostly wait.*

```java
// WRONG for coordination: sleep keeps the lock the whole time
synchronized (lock) {
    Thread.sleep(5000);   // lock is HELD for all 5 seconds
    process();            // no other thread can enter this block meanwhile
}
```

```java
// RIGHT: wait() releases the lock so other threads can make progress
synchronized (lock) {
    while (!ready) {
        lock.wait();      // releases lock and parks until notify()
    }
    process();            // re-acquires the lock automatically before continuing
}
```

sleep is a static timed pause that keeps locks; wait releases the monitor and needs notify() to resume.
